// Fides AprilTag detection (robotics / perception)
//
// Detection of AprilTag tag36h11 markers (the family ROS, OpenCV and most
// robots use), on stdlib/vision:
//   - the tag36h11 family: all 587 codes and the bit layout (bit i at cell
//     (bit_x[i], bit_y[i]) of the 8x8 grid inside the white border, most
//     significant bit first), copied from AprilTag 3's tag36h11.c (see the
//     notice below); a code turned by 90 degrees through that layout;
//   - rendering a tag (10 x 10 cells: white border, black border, 6 x 6
//     data) into an image through any homography, with 4x4 supersampling,
//     to build test scenes; the homography of a tag at a known pose;
//   - detection: Otsu threshold, dark connected components, the outer
//     boundary of each (flood fill of the outside within its bounding box),
//     sub-pixel boundary points (interpolated threshold crossings), four
//     corners from extreme points, refined by total-least-squares lines
//     through each side, the homography from the tag square [-1, 1]^2 to
//     the corners (vision's DLT), cell sampling (bilinear), a threshold
//     between the black border and the white quiet zone, a check of the
//     black border, and the 6 x 6 data bits in all four rotations matched
//     to the code table by Hamming distance;
//   - pose from the homography with a known tag size and camera (corners
//     undistorted first): H in normalized coordinates ~ [s r1, s r2, t],
//     scale from the first two columns, r1 and r2 made orthonormal
//     symmetrically, r3 = r1 x r2; the tag frame has x right, y down and
//     z into the tag, as in AprilTag.
// Corners are reported in the tag's own order (AprilTag's): the corners at
// tag coordinates (-1,-1), (1,-1), (1,1), (-1,1).
// Not done: the decimation, adaptive thresholding and gradient-based
// clustering of the full AprilTag 3 detector, so this needs fair contrast
// and tags apart from each other. No platform seam: images come from
// stdlib/vision.
//
// The tag36h11 code table and bit layout are from AprilTag
// (https://github.com/AprilRobotics/apriltag, tag36h11.c):
//   Copyright (C) 2013-2016, The Regents of The University of Michigan.
//   All rights reserved. This software was developed in the APRIL Robotics
//   Lab under the direction of Edwin Olson, ebolson@umich.edu.
//   Redistribution and use in source and binary forms, with or without
//   modification, are permitted provided that the following conditions are
//   met: 1. Redistributions of source code must retain the above copyright
//   notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer.
//   2. Redistributions in binary form must reproduce the above copyright
//   notice, this list of conditions and the following disclaimer in the
//   documentation and/or other materials provided with the distribution.
//   THIS SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED BY THE COPYRIGHT HOLDERS AND CONTRIBUTORS "AS
//   IS" AND ANY EXPRESS OR IMPLIED WARRANTIES, INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED
//   TO, THE IMPLIED WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY AND FITNESS FOR A
//   PARTICULAR PURPOSE ARE DISCLAIMED. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE COPYRIGHT OWNER
//   OR CONTRIBUTORS BE LIABLE FOR ANY DIRECT, INDIRECT, INCIDENTAL, SPECIAL,
//   EXEMPLARY, OR CONSEQUENTIAL DAMAGES (INCLUDING, BUT NOT LIMITED TO,
//   PROCUREMENT OF SUBSTITUTE GOODS OR SERVICES; LOSS OF USE, DATA, OR
//   PROFITS; OR BUSINESS INTERRUPTION) HOWEVER CAUSED AND ON ANY THEORY OF
//   LIABILITY, WHETHER IN CONTRACT, STRICT LIABILITY, OR TORT (INCLUDING
//   NEGLIGENCE OR OTHERWISE) ARISING IN ANY WAY OUT OF THE USE OF THIS
//   SOFTWARE, EVEN IF ADVISED OF THE POSSIBILITY OF SUCH DAMAGE.
//
// Usage:
//   use apriltag;
//   val dets: Vec<AtagDetection> = atag_detect(&gray, 2);
//   val pose: AtagPose = atag_pose(&dets.get(0), &camera, 0.16);

module apriltag;
use math;
use quat;
use vision;

// ─── The tag36h11 family ───

var atag_table: Vec<i64> = Vec::new();

